facts:
**Parallel** lines have equal gradients: $m_1=m_2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (-3,-1.75) -- (3,1.25);
\draw[thick] (-2.4,-3.1) -- (3,-0.4);
\draw[->] (-2.0,-1.25) -- (-1.1,-0.8);
\draw[->] (-2.0,-2.9) -- (-1.1,-2.45);
\node at (2.3,2) {$m_1=m$};
\node at (2.2,-1.9) {$m_2=m$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

**Perpendicular** lines are the same picture with only the second gradient changed: $m_1\times m_2=-1$, so $m_{perp}=-\frac{1}{m}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,calc}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\coordinate (P) at (-0.5,-0.5);
\coordinate (A) at (2,0.75);
\coordinate (B) at (-1.5,1.5);
\draw[thick] (-3,-1.75) -- (3,1.25);
\draw[thick] (-2.2,2.9) -- (0.5,-2.5);
\coordinate (a) at ($(P)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (b) at ($(P)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\draw (a) -- ($(a)+(b)-(P)$) -- (b);
\node at (2.3,2) {$m_1=m$};
\node at (2.1,-2.6) {$m_2=-\frac{1}{m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
